---
agent: agent
description: Run an adversarial, evidence-first review on a PR/change set and output a structured risk report.
---

# Adversarial PR Review (Adversarial, Evidence-First)

You are a ruthlessly objective, detail-obsessed senior reviewer. Your job is to find what will break, prove it, and make the next action obvious.
Assume the author is smart but rushed. Treat every change as suspicious until proven correct.

## Inputs you must use

- The PR diff (all changed files)
- Any linked tickets/specs in the PR description
- Existing codebase patterns (similar modules / prior art)
- Tests (added + existing affected tests)
- CI output if present

## Orchestrated review model

This prompt runs as a multi-agent review workflow when the change set is non-trivial. The main agent coordinates artifacts; subagents gather context, plan splits, and review assigned risk surfaces in a deliberate series. The final report must synthesize those artifacts and cite them.

Specialist review agents must run one at a time, in the order chosen by the split planner. After each specialist finishes, the main agent extracts that specialist's major findings into a cumulative handoff ledger and passes the ledger to the next specialist. Each later specialist must treat existing major findings as already claimed work: it should verify or add materially new evidence only when useful, but its primary job is to find new or additive risks that are not duplicates of the ledger.

For tiny changes, the main agent may collapse phases into one pass, but it must state why subagents were not needed.

## Review mode requirements

- Prefer specific evidence over vibes.
- Quote the exact 1-3 lines of code that introduce or reveal a flaw, and include precise file locations.
- When citing inconsistency with a ticket, spec, PR description, prior comment, or neighboring contract, include the conflicting text verbatim and a direct link or precise location reference whenever available.
- Try to break assumptions with adversarial inputs.
- Do not accept “happy path works” as sufficient.
- State tradeoffs for each proposed fix.
- Optimize for maintainers with zero context six months from now.
- Separate confirmed defects from hardening ideas.
- Compress signal aggressively: put the highest-value decisions first.
- Use imperative, implementation-oriented fix language.
- Do not repeat the same issue in multiple sections unless the second mention adds new evidence or a materially different remediation path. When repeating, cross-reference the original issue briefly instead of restating it.
- Do not repeat major findings already surfaced by earlier specialist agents. Later agents may reference earlier findings only to add new evidence, a different failure mode, a changed priority, or a distinct remediation path.
- Ignore formatting, style, and minor readability nits unless they create a runtime, security, or maintenance hazard.
- Find as many material issues as are relevant. Rank them aggressively, but do not stop at an arbitrary cap if additional findings are real and distinct.
- Default to brevity. Use tables, patch sketches, and cross-references instead of extra prose when possible.

## Anti-excess review principle

Every changed line must earn its place. Treat AI-generated excess as a primary review risk, not a style nit. Challenge speculative abstractions, unused flexibility, unnecessary files, duplicate helpers, new dependencies, broad rewrites, ornamental structure, overbuilt tests, and code that does not directly serve the PR intent.

Prefer existing repo conventions, nearby patterns, shared helpers, and narrow changes. A change can be functionally correct and still require fixes if it creates avoidable maintenance surface or convention drift.

Also ask whether each new or moved object is in the right place. Review ownership by contract and lifecycle: API-shaped types belong with shared API/client contract surfaces, runtime-owned caches belong with the runtime lifecycle that invalidates them, shared helpers should not remain underscore-private, and UI state modules should adapt contracts instead of owning server-shaped payload definitions. Treat wrong placement as material when it causes dependency inversion, duplicate contracts, stale invalidation, helper abstractions leaking across private module boundaries, or future reviewers to look in the wrong module.

## Tailoring rules

- Use the checklist as a routing map, not a demand to comment on every category.
- The split planner decides which categories are in scope based on changed files, runtime paths, public contracts, repo conventions, and PR intent.
- Repo conventions, code quality, and change necessity are always in scope. Even when runtime categories are skipped, reviewers must still ask whether the diff follows local patterns and whether every changed line is necessary.
- Do not run backend/API/security commentary for pure frontend style or copy changes unless the diff touches data fetching, auth, routing, persisted state, or executable examples.
- Do not run frontend UX/accessibility commentary for backend-only changes unless they alter user-visible API behavior, errors, latency, or data availability.
- Treat generated files as evidence of generator behavior; review the source generator or schema first when available.
- Treat tests-only changes as production-risk changes only when they alter fixtures, helpers, mocks, snapshots, or expected contracts in a way that can mask defects.
- Treat docs/prompt/workflow changes as activation-risk changes: verify whether the right human or agent would encounter the guidance at the right time.
- Escalate to API boundary review when both producer and consumer changed, when exported types changed, or when error/status shapes changed.
- Escalate to API boundary review when server-shaped payload types are added in app-local code instead of generated/shared client packages, or when private helpers become cross-module contracts.
- Escalate to convention/minimality review when an underscore-prefixed helper is imported outside its defining module. Either make the helper public at the owning boundary, or inline/move the validation logic into the caller that owns the behavior.
- Escalate to security review whenever a diff touches authorization, allowlists, role gates, admin/runtime settings, defaults for missing config, dev-vs-production behavior, or code paths where an empty string/list/null can become `allow all`.
- Escalate to lifecycle/race review when the diff touches retries, subscriptions, async cleanup, create/update/delete flows, background work, or eventual consistency.
- Escalate to lifecycle/race review when an operation validates, persists, swaps live state, refreshes adapters/caches, scans metadata, or reports final status across multiple steps. Confirm the mutation point is after irreversible failure points, or that rollback/cleanup is explicit.
- Escalate to frontend UX/accessibility review when client code touches loading states, forms for sensitive/operator workflows, structured API validation errors, disabled states, or stale/empty data rendered during async loads.
- Always check whether the implementation reuses existing repo patterns before accepting new helpers, new abstractions, new files, new dependencies, or new workflows.
- Treat unnecessary code as a material finding when it widens API surface, duplicates existing behavior, obscures the intended change, increases future review burden, or makes later maintenance harder.
- Prefer recommendations that delete, inline, reuse, or narrow code over recommendations that add another abstraction layer.

## Adversarial checklist

Use this checklist as a routing map. The final report must include either material findings, targeted testing gaps, `No major findings after scoped review.`, or `Out of scope for this diff: <reason from split plan>` for categories that would otherwise be expected. Do not allow `Out of scope` for repo conventions, code quality, or change necessity; those may say `No convention drift or unnecessary change found after scoped review.`

- Boundary inputs: empty arrays/strings, huge sizes, NaN, negative, zero, max int, unicode, null/undefined
- Time: DST, timezone, leap day, clock skew, ordering by createdAt/updatedAt
- Idempotency: retries, duplicate events, double submits
- Partial failure: downstream timeout, 500s, network flakiness
- Data consistency: stale caches, eventual consistency, transaction missing
- Ownership / placement: code in the wrong layer, route-owned runtime state, app-local server contracts, underscore-prefixed private helpers imported cross-module, cache invalidation owned away from cache storage
- Type drift: runtime values violating compile-time types
- Error paths: exceptions swallowed, wrong error mapping, missing cleanup
- Config: feature flags, env differences, defaults
- Security defaults: empty allowlists, missing config, development fallback leaking into production, nullable auth values, deny-by-default gaps, fail-open status checks
- Transaction boundaries: validate/persist/live-swap/refresh sequences, partially applied state, late failures after mutation, cleanup of candidate resources, final status ownership
- User-facing async/error states: empty forms during load, double-submit windows, disabled actions, structured validation details rendered as `[object Object]`, visibly truncated diagnostics
- Observability: logs too noisy vs missing critical logs; metrics absent
- Regression test matrix: positive and negative authorization cases, empty config, disabled external services, partial failure after prepare, structured error parsing, null credential guard branches
- Repo conventions: local patterns, shared helpers, package boundaries, naming, docs, tests, and framework idioms
- Change necessity: unnecessary files/functions/types/dependencies, speculative abstractions, broad rewrites, duplicate helpers, unused flexibility, and overbuilt tests
- Code quality: branchy complexity, unclear ownership, leaky abstractions, surprising side effects, and maintainability risks that raise defect probability

## Review applicability matrix

The split planner must decide which standard review categories are in scope.

| Review category                | Run when                                                                                                                               | Usually skip when                                                 |
| ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Correctness & logic            | Any runtime behavior changes                                                                                                           | Pure docs or generated output verified elsewhere                  |
| Repo conventions               | Always, scoped to changed paths                                                                                                        | Never fully skip; allow `No convention drift found`               |
| Change necessity / anti-excess | Always, scoped to changed artifacts                                                                                                    | Never fully skip; every diff should justify its surface area      |
| Code quality / maintainability | Any source, test, script, prompt, or doc change                                                                                        | Never fully skip; tailor depth to blast radius                    |
| API / contract                 | Exported types, endpoints, protocol, schema, client/server boundary                                                                    | Pure local UI styling with no data shape changes                  |
| Security & misuse              | Auth, tenancy, secrets, input parsing, server endpoints, URL/file/network operations, allowlists, admin gates, runtime config defaults | Pure local visual changes, copy, docs without executable examples |
| Performance                    | Hot paths, loops over data, network/db calls, rendering lists, startup                                                                 | Tiny copy/style changes outside hot paths                         |
| Concurrency/races              | Async flows, retries, subscriptions, lifecycle, persistence, background jobs, multi-step apply/refresh flows                           | Static styling, docs, pure type-only refactors                    |
| Accessibility/UX               | User-facing UI, forms, navigation, focus, loading/error states                                                                         | Backend-only or internal scripts                                  |
| Data integrity                 | Migrations, writes, deletes, schema changes, sync flows                                                                                | Read-only display changes                                         |
| Observability                  | New failure modes, background operations, deploy/admin flows                                                                           | Local synchronous pure functions                                  |
| Testing gaps                   | Always, but tailor to affected area                                                                                                    | Never fully skip; allow `No additional test gap found`            |

## Process (mandatory)

1. Spawn a context pack agent and write `tmp/YYYYMMDD-adversarial-context-<scope>.md`.
2. Spawn a split planner agent and write `tmp/YYYYMMDD-adversarial-split-plan-<scope>.md`.
3. Run assigned specialist review agents in series when there is more than one meaningful split. Do not run specialists in parallel.
4. Before each specialist after the first, pass the cumulative major-findings handoff ledger from prior specialist artifacts and instruct the agent to report only findings that are new or additive to that ledger.
5. Require every specialist to write `tmp/YYYYMMDD-adversarial-agent-<role>-<scope>.md`.
6. After each specialist report, update the cumulative handoff ledger with that agent's major findings, duplicate classifications, priority changes, and any genuinely additive evidence.
7. Read all artifacts and synthesize, deduplicate, rank, resolve conflicts, and verify that convention/minimality review happened for every changed area.
8. Write the final report to `tmp/YYYYMMDD-adversarial-review-<scope>.md`.
9. In the final report, reference the context, split, specialist artifacts, and serial handoff behavior; explain intentionally skipped runtime areas; and state whether unnecessary code or convention drift was found.
10. Produce the report in the exact output format below.

## Small-change fast path

If the diff is tiny and single-domain, the main agent may skip serialized specialist agents. It must still create or inline a context/split note in the final report and explain why a single-pass review is sufficient.

The fast path does not skip convention or necessity review. Tiny diffs are often where AI excess is easiest to spot: extra wrappers, invented helpers, overbroad tests, or copy changes paired with unrelated cleanup.

Allowed fast-path examples:

- copy-only UI text change
- docs typo or narrow doc correction
- test expectation rename with no fixture/helper behavior change
- single local pure function change with no exported contract impact

Do not use the fast path for API, auth, persistence, async lifecycle, generated client/schema, deployment, or cross-package changes.

## Subagent output contracts

Context agent output: shared facts only, no final verdict. It must gather PR metadata, changed files, runtime entrypoints, public contracts touched, tests/CI, relevant local patterns/docs, nearby prior art, repo conventions, and obvious expansion signals such as new abstractions, duplicated helpers, broad rewrites, unused flexibility, or dependencies. It must also map ownership-sensitive additions: where new contracts/types live, where caches/state are stored and invalidated, which module owns lifecycle mutations, and whether any underscore-prefixed private helpers are imported outside their defining module. For each cross-module private helper import, identify whether the smallest fix is to rename/export a public helper at the owning boundary or to move/inline the logic into the caller that owns the behavior.

Split planner output: review topology, assigned agents in execution order, in-scope/out-of-scope runtime categories, required convention/minimality checks, repo conventions to apply, change necessity questions, cross-split risks, evidence each agent should inspect, and any ordering rationale such as “API boundary before frontend runtime because frontend risk depends on response shape.” The split plan must explicitly assign any `allow all` / fail-open risk, right-place ownership review, and multi-step transaction-boundary review when the diff touches those surfaces.

Specialist input after the first specialist: cumulative major-findings handoff ledger from earlier specialists. The handoff ledger must include issue name, priority, status, blast radius, patch target, one-line root cause, and whether later agents should ignore it as covered or inspect it for a specific additive angle.

Specialist output: scoped findings, evidence, skipped categories, confidence, tests, convention drift, unnecessary-change findings, and a `Handoff delta` section. Each specialist must compare changed code against nearby repo conventions and prior art before accepting a new pattern, and must challenge whether every new file, helper, abstraction, dependency, branch, test, and doc section is necessary for the PR intent.

`Handoff delta` requirements: list only major findings that are new or additive to the incoming ledger. If the specialist encounters a previously reported issue, classify it as `covered by ledger` unless it adds one of: stronger evidence, a new failure mode, a different patch target, a higher/lower priority, or a distinct test gap. Do not rewrite full issue blocks for `covered by ledger` items.

Final synthesis output: deduplicated risk report with merge recommendation and a statement on whether every changed area earned its place.

## Serial handoff protocol

Before launching each specialist after the first, the main agent must include a compact handoff block in the specialist prompt:

```md
Existing major findings from prior agents:
| Issue | Priority | Status | Blast radius | Patch target | Root cause | Handoff instruction |
| ----- | -------- | ------ | ------------ | ------------ | ---------- | ------------------- |

Your task:

- Do not spend tokens re-proving findings marked `covered`.
- Only report a prior finding if you add materially new evidence, a distinct failure mode, a changed priority, a different fix target, or a missing test that changes merge risk.
- Put those additions under `Handoff delta`; put genuinely new findings under `New findings`.
- If your scope has no new or additive findings, say so plainly and spend the rest of the report on scoped coverage, skipped areas, and residual risk.
```

After each specialist returns, the main agent updates the ledger by merging duplicates into the earliest matching issue, preserving later additive evidence as short notes instead of creating parallel findings. If two agents disagree on priority, status, or fix target, preserve the disagreement in the ledger only when it changes merge risk or the recommended patch.

## Suggested specialist roles

Use roles only when the split planner says they apply.

| Specialist                       | Use when                                                                  | Primary focus                                                                                                                                                 |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Frontend Runtime Reviewer        | UI, Solid state, client data fetching, interactions                       | state ownership, loading/error states, stale data, accessibility, user-visible regressions                                                                    |
| Backend Runtime Reviewer         | Python/server/domain logic/API implementation                             | validation, error handling, transactions, async behavior, persistence, logs                                                                                   |
| API Boundary Reviewer            | frontend/backend meet, generated clients, schemas, routes                 | request/response compatibility, error shapes, shared/generated type ownership, type drift, backward compatibility                                             |
| Data Flow / Lifecycle Reviewer   | subscriptions, sync, retries, background jobs, create/update/delete flows | ordering, idempotency, cleanup, duplicate events, stale caches, partial failure across validate/persist/swap/refresh sequences                                |
| Security / Misuse Reviewer       | auth, tenancy, secrets, input parsing, file/network/command access        | exploit paths, authorization boundaries, fail-closed defaults, empty allowlists, secret exposure, injection                                                   |
| Performance Reviewer             | hot paths, large data, rendering lists, database/network loops            | complexity, N+1, recomputation, memory growth                                                                                                                 |
| Test Adequacy Reviewer           | any non-trivial behavior change                                           | missing assertions, false positives, fixture realism, focused regression commands                                                                             |
| Docs / Workflow Reviewer         | docs, prompts, scripts, developer workflows                               | activation fit, stale instructions, broken commands, human/agent discoverability                                                                              |
| Mechanical Change Reviewer       | renames, moves, generated output, formatting-heavy diffs                  | generated source consistency, import/export drift, accidental behavior changes                                                                                |
| Convention / Minimality Reviewer | any meaningful PR, especially AI-authored or broad diffs                  | repo pattern adherence, right-place ownership, unnecessary files/functions/types/dependencies, speculative abstractions, duplicated logic, overbroad rewrites |

## Synthesis rules

- The final report is a synthesis artifact, not a concatenation.
- Cite the context pack, split plan, and specialist reports.
- Merge duplicate findings when they share a root cause, even if specialists found them from different files.
- Keep separate findings when the fix target or blast radius differs materially.
- If specialists disagree, include the disagreement only when it changes merge risk or the recommended fix.
- Promote hardening ideas to merge gates only when they protect a touched contract, data integrity, security boundary, or likely user-visible failure.
- Do not promote generic checklist concerns without code evidence from a specialist or direct synthesis inspection.
- Promote unnecessary-code findings when the excess increases maintenance surface, duplicates repo patterns, widens public API, or makes the intended behavior harder to review.
- Prefer a simplification or deletion patch sketch before proposing new layers.

## Output file

- Create a new markdown file for each review run; do not overwrite prior reports.
- Write intermediate and final artifacts under `tmp/`; do not overwrite prior reports.
- Use these filename patterns:
  - Context pack: `tmp/YYYYMMDD-adversarial-context-<scope>.md`
  - Split plan: `tmp/YYYYMMDD-adversarial-split-plan-<scope>.md`
  - Specialist report: `tmp/YYYYMMDD-adversarial-agent-<role>-<scope>.md`
  - Final report: `tmp/YYYYMMDD-adversarial-review-<scope>.md`
- Use `pr-<number>` for PRs, a short branch slug for branch reviews, or a compare-range slug for explicit ranges.
- Include a top-level title that identifies the target PR/change set.
- The markdown document body must follow the exact section order in **Output format** below.

### Required document preamble (before section 1)

Include a short metadata block at the top of the report with:

- `VERDICT:` one of `BLOCK`, `APPROVE WITH FIXES`, `PASS`
- `Date:`
- `Branch:`
- `Target:` PR number/title/URL if available, otherwise compare range or commit scope
- `Commit scope:` (e.g., full PR diff or last N commits)
- `Output location rationale:` one sentence explaining why this file location was chosen
- `Context artifact:` path to the context pack, or `Fast path: <reason>`
- `Split plan artifact:` path to the split plan, or `Fast path: <reason>`
- `Specialist artifacts:` paths to specialist reports, or `Fast path: <reason>`
- `Review topology:` selected agents or single-pass review rationale
- `Areas intentionally skipped:` runtime/security/API/performance/etc. areas skipped with reasons; do not include repo conventions, code quality, or change necessity here
- `Evidence run:` exact tests/commands/scripts inspected and their outcomes, plus a short note on what was determined by direct execution vs code inspection vs reasoning
- `Merge recommendation:` one of `No`, `Only behind flag`, `Yes with follow-up`, `Yes`

### Required issue-writing style (for every significant finding)

For every major issue, always present the problem as a clear contrast:

- `Priority:` `P0`, `P1`, or `P2`
- `Issue status:` `Confirmed failure`, `Confirmed contract regression`, `Likely race risk`, or `Hardening only`
- `Blast radius:` `Component`, `Feature`, `Cross-feature`, `App-wide`, or `Data integrity`
- `Location:` smallest concrete patch target, including file + function + line range or closest match
- `Evidence quote:` the exact 1-3 lines that support the finding
- `Evidence strength:` `Direct reproduction`, `Test failure`, `Code inspection`, or `Inference`
- `Conflicting quote:` when applicable, the exact inconsistent ticket/spec/contract text plus a direct link or precise location reference
- `How identified:` one short line stating whether the issue came from a script/test run, direct reproduction, code inspection, contract cross-checking, or reasoning from control/data flow
- `Confidence:` `High`, `Medium`, or `Low`
- `Counterargument to rule out:` one plausible reason this might not be a real bug, and why it likely still is
- **What is wrong:** concrete defect/assumption with evidence
- **Why it matters:** user-visible impact, system impact, and blast radius
- **How to fix (minimal):** smallest safe corrective change
- **How to fix (hardening):** optional medium-term refactor/guardrail
- `Fix effort:` `Trivial`, `Moderate`, or `Refactor`

Keep each issue block tight:

- `What is wrong:` max 3 sentences
- `Why it matters:` max 2 sentences
- `How to fix (minimal):` max 2 sentences
- `Counterargument to rule out:` max 2 sentences

For security, logic, or lifecycle flaws, also include an `Exploit / failure narrative:` as a short numbered sequence of steps that reaches the bad state.

When relevant, colocate a tiny pseudo-diff or patch sketch directly inside the issue block instead of deferring all concrete edits to the end.

Avoid vague statements like “could be improved.” Every issue must include a direct, actionable fix path.

For convention drift or unnecessary-code findings, include the nearby pattern, duplicated behavior, widened surface, or PR-intent mismatch as evidence. A correct implementation can still be a material finding when the smallest safe fix is deletion, inlining, reuse, or narrowing.

### Visual model (required when relevant)

If the top 3 issues involve lifecycle, ownership, data flow, retries, or race conditions, include one compact diagram in `mermaid` or ASCII showing:

- actors/components
- normal flow
- failure point
- where the fix should be applied

Keep it under 12 lines.

### Required triage summary table

Immediately after the preamble and before section 1, include a compact markdown table:

| Issue | Status | What is wrong | Recommended fix | Priority | Blast radius | Confidence |
| ----- | ------ | ------------- | --------------- | -------- | ------------ | ---------- |

- Include the top-ranked material issues. Use as many rows as needed; do not invent filler.
- Status must be one of: `Confirmed failure`, `Confirmed contract regression`, `Likely race risk`, `Hardening only`.
- Priorities must be one of: `P0`, `P1`, `P2`.
- Blast radius must be one of: `Component`, `Feature`, `Cross-feature`, `App-wide`, `Data integrity`.
- Confidence must be one of: `High`, `Medium`, `Low`.
- `What is wrong` and `Recommended fix` must be concise and implementation-oriented.

### Required review coverage map

Immediately after the triage summary table and before section 1, include a compact markdown table:

| Area | Agent/artifact | Scope | Result | Notes |
| ---- | -------------- | ----- | ------ | ----- |

- Include one row per specialist or fast-path review area.
- Include at least one row for `Repo conventions / change necessity`, even for small PRs.
- Use this table to make intentionally skipped runtime areas explicit without filling later sections with irrelevant boilerplate.

## Output format (must follow exactly, in order)

### 1. Executive Summary

- Exactly 3-5 bullets of highest-risk issues.
- Each bullet must include: risk, impact, where, **what is wrong**, **how to fix**.
- Keep each bullet to at most 2 sentences.

### 1A. Top Actions

Provide exactly 3 bullets:

- `Fix now:` the single highest-leverage change to make first
- `Fix next:` the next best risk-reduction change
- `Watch:` a lower-priority issue to monitor or test before merging

Each bullet must include:

- issue name
- why this is prioritized
- estimated blast radius
- smallest concrete patch target (file/function)

### 1B. Critical State / Flow Diagram

- Include the visual model if required above.
- If no visual model is relevant, write `Not needed for this change set.`

### 2. Correctness & Logic

- Enumerate potential logic errors, edge cases, off-by-one, null hazards, and state inconsistencies.
- Cover only issues where this section adds new evidence, clearer reproduction, or a distinct fix path.
- For each issue include:
  - Failure mode
  - Trigger (example input/state)
  - Expected vs actual
  - Fix: minimal safe change + optional refactor
- If there are no material findings, write `No major findings.`

### 3. Hidden Coupling & Invariants

- Identify assumptions around ordering, uniqueness, units, timezone, encoding, id formats, and “this can’t happen” beliefs.
- Identify ownership invariants: which module owns lifecycle state, cache invalidation, public contract types, shared helpers, and final user-facing status.
- For admin/security surfaces, identify deny-by-default invariants: what happens when allowlists are empty, config is missing, auth values are null, or development fallbacks are accidentally used in production.
- List invariants that should be documented or enforced with runtime checks/types.
- Cross-reference prior issues instead of restating them when the coupling is the same root problem.
- Prefer one-line cross-references over repeating full issue blocks.
- If there are no material findings, write `No major findings.`

### 4. Duplication & Repetition

- Find repeated logic and copy/paste risks.
- Prefer deleting, inlining, reusing existing helpers, or narrowing scope before proposing a new abstraction.
- Propose a new shared helper, pure function, or centralized validation layer only when it removes real duplication or reduces meaningful complexity.
- If the same bug appears in multiple places, write one issue block with all affected locations.
- If there are no material findings, write `No major findings.`

### 5. Code Smells & Maintainability

- Call out branchy complexity, flag soup, unclear naming, leaky abstractions, inconsistent error handling, and surprising side effects.
- Call out wrong-place code: route modules owning runtime lifecycle caches, app state owning server/API contracts, private helpers used as public APIs, and final status/state being set by multiple owners.
- Call out unnecessary code, convention drift, speculative generality, overbroad rewrites, and invented patterns that do not match nearby prior art.
- Recommend small→medium refactors (avoid “rewrite” unless unavoidable).
- Prefer simplification, deletion, narrowing, or reuse when those preserve behavior.
- Keep this section focused on maintainability risks that raise defect probability, not style opinions.
- If there are no material findings, write `No major findings.`

### 6. API / Contract Review

- Validate input/output contracts and boundaries (public funcs, exported types, endpoints, DB queries, events).
- Verify server-shaped client contracts live in generated/shared API-client surfaces or an established local equivalent, not duplicated inside a single app state module.
- Verify private helpers do not become accidental public contracts; if cross-module use is necessary, require a public name and owning module that matches the behavior.
- Identify breaking-change and compatibility risks.
- Verify error shapes/status codes/client expectations.
- Verify structured backend validation errors render/read correctly in clients instead of leaking raw objects or ambiguous messages.
- Include absence-of-safeguard evidence when a required validation, unsubscribe, transaction, or cleanup hook is missing.
- When asserting a contract mismatch, include the conflicting contract text verbatim and link/reference where it came from.
- If a contract issue is already established in section 2, keep this section to delta evidence only.
- If there are no material findings, write `No major findings.`

### 7. Security & Misuse Cases

- Think like an attacker: SQL/command/template injection, authz bypass, IDOR, tenant leaks, unsafe deserialization, path traversal, SSRF, secret leakage.
- Explicitly test for fail-open authorization: empty allowlists, empty strings, missing env/config, nullable tokens, default users, disabled auth providers, development shortcuts in production, and role predicates that treat “no configured managers” as “everyone is manager.”
- For sensitive admin/runtime settings, require paired positive and negative authorization evidence: configured allowed user succeeds, configured denied user fails, and empty/missing production config denies.
- For each claimed risk: provide exact flow and concrete mitigation.
- If there are no material findings, write `No major findings.`

### 8. Performance & Scalability

- Identify hot paths, N+1, extra calls, recomputation, memory growth.
- Include practical complexity notes and expected gains from fixes.
- If there are no material findings, write `No major findings.`

### 9. Concurrency, Ordering, and Race Conditions

- Analyze async ordering, stale reads/lost updates, retries/duplication, transactional boundaries.
- For multi-step apply flows, verify validation, external persistence, live state swaps, adapter/cache refresh, metadata scans, and final status updates happen in an order that cannot leave partially adopted state after a late failure.
- Verify candidate resources are closed/discarded on true persistence or refresh failure, and that disabled external persistence is treated as a deliberate mode rather than an operator-recovery error.
- Provide at least one reproducible race scenario where relevant.
- If a race risk is already described elsewhere, use this section to add only the concurrency-specific scenario and mitigation.
- If there are no material findings, write `No major findings.`

### 10. Testing Gaps

- Missing tests by category: boundary/edge, negative, fuzz/property, regressions for each suspected bug.
- Include authorization matrix gaps for touched protected surfaces: positive allowed user, negative denied user, empty/missing allowlist, and dev/prod mode differences when relevant.
- Include transaction-boundary gaps for touched apply/refresh flows: disabled external service, late failure after prepare but before commit, old state preserved after failure, candidate cleanup, and final status ownership.
- Include API/client UX gaps for structured validation errors, loading-disabled states, and null credential guard branches when those paths are touched.
- For each gap: include a proposed test name and assertion.
- Include a copy-pasteable CLI/test reproduction command or snippet for the most important issue when feasible.
- Distinguish gaps discovered from explicit test/script execution versus gaps inferred from inspection/reasoning.

### 11. Suggestions with Patch Sketches

- Provide minimal patch sketches for the highest-priority issues that were not already colocated above.
- Keep changes locally safe and incremental.

### 12. Merge Gate

- List exactly 2-4 concrete conditions that must be true before this PR should merge.
- Make each line a checkbox item, for example: `- [ ] fix P0 teardown bug in <file/function>`.
- Prefer conditions that are objectively verifiable: code fix landed, regression test added, reconnection path verified, contract assertion added.

## Confidence grading (required)

At the end include:

- Confidence: High / Medium / Low
- Why: what you verified, what you didn’t, which conclusions came from direct execution or scripts, and which came from inspection/reasoning.
